<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Guaira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Guaira is the main port of Venezuela, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast just north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, from which it is separated by the coastal mountain range. Fortified in the colonial era, it handled much of the country's trade, including cocoa exports. In 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a> was arrested there and handed over to the Spanish, and in February 1814 Bolívar ordered the execution of hundreds of Spanish prisoners held in the port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-guaira.jpg" alt="La Guaira" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karla García Fernández · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Guaira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
